WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.841
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:06.111 --> 00:08.112
[theme music playing]

3
00:48.111 --> 00:55.076
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

4
00:56.995 --> 00:59.289
[soft music playing]

5
01:01.082 --> 01:02.208
It's Kim Tae-ung.

6
01:04.794 --> 01:05.795
My Joseon name.

7
01:07.255 --> 01:08.298
Kim

8
01:09.591 --> 01:10.925
Tae-ung.

9
01:13.303 --> 01:14.304
I like you.

10
01:25.565 --> 01:27.567
I like you, Ms. Keum.

11
01:39.120 --> 01:40.163
Hmm?

12
01:42.248 --> 01:43.249
I'm lying.

13
01:44.834 --> 01:45.668
What?

14
01:45.752 --> 01:47.503
You lied to me too

15
01:48.546 --> 01:50.214
when you said you liked me.

16
01:53.343 --> 01:54.636
Oh?

17
01:54.719 --> 01:56.638
But that wasn't a lie.

18
01:57.138 --> 01:59.265
I mean, that was a lie, but…

19
01:59.349 --> 02:02.227
Mr. Sato, where are you going?

20
02:02.310 --> 02:03.478
Mr. Sato.

21
02:03.561 --> 02:04.437
Mr…

22
02:04.979 --> 02:06.231
[screams] Hey, Kim Tae-ung!

23
02:06.731 --> 02:07.732
[gasps]

24
02:08.942 --> 02:09.943
[groans]

25
02:13.780 --> 02:14.781
[coughs]

26
02:15.490 --> 02:16.324
[groans]

27
02:16.407 --> 02:17.283
Was that a dream?

28
02:18.701 --> 02:21.329
[yawns]

29
02:21.412 --> 02:22.455
[inhales]

30
02:23.289 --> 02:24.207
Hmm?

31
02:29.545 --> 02:31.381
Did you sleep well?

32
02:33.299 --> 02:34.300
Oh…

33
02:34.926 --> 02:35.927
[clears throat]

34
02:36.844 --> 02:38.137
Good morning.

35
02:41.224 --> 02:43.268
Why did I sleep here?

36
02:45.144 --> 02:46.145
Don't you remember?

37
02:46.729 --> 02:47.939
Remember what?

38
02:50.108 --> 02:51.317
Ah!

39
02:51.401 --> 02:54.028
I had a beer with Sato Hideo

40
02:54.112 --> 02:57.156
after work yesterday…

41
03:00.410 --> 03:01.744
[stammers] Two beers, I guess?

42
03:02.745 --> 03:04.998
I think I had about that much.

43
03:05.623 --> 03:06.624
[scoffs]

44
03:07.125 --> 03:09.335
I don't remember anything after that.

45
03:11.421 --> 03:13.756
I swear. I don't remember anything at all.

46
03:13.840 --> 03:17.927
So… how did I get home yesterday?

47
03:23.016 --> 03:24.934
[jazz music playing]

48
03:25.935 --> 03:27.520
[Hideo] It's Kim Tae-ung.

49
03:28.521 --> 03:29.439
My Joseon name.

50
03:30.231 --> 03:31.065
Kim

51
03:32.150 --> 03:33.109
Tae-ung.

52
03:36.696 --> 03:37.530
[groans softly]

53
03:40.742 --> 03:41.659
Ms. Keum.

54
03:44.620 --> 03:45.580
Ms. Keum?

55
03:50.335 --> 03:51.669
Wake up, Ms. Keum.

56
03:55.757 --> 03:56.883
Ms. Lee Gae-ttong.

57
03:59.510 --> 04:00.345
What the…

58
04:01.262 --> 04:03.181
She passed out just like that?

59
04:18.029 --> 04:19.864
[doorbell buzzing]

60
04:27.663 --> 04:29.916
EPISODE 8

61
04:34.587 --> 04:35.588
[Hideo groans]

62
04:37.632 --> 04:38.633
[Ga-kyung moans]

63
04:40.968 --> 04:42.720
Can you explain what happened?

64
04:42.804 --> 04:46.474
We had a few beers to build team spirit in the Foreign Affairs Division.

65
04:46.557 --> 04:49.727
Ms. Keum got drunk, so I tried to take her home by rickshaw,

66
04:50.269 --> 04:52.605
but one of the wheels fell off on the way.

67
04:52.688 --> 04:53.815
That's what happened.

68
04:55.483 --> 04:56.609
It's true.

69
04:56.692 --> 04:57.568
[grunts]

70
05:00.613 --> 05:02.865
But wouldn't you normally carry her on your back?

71
05:05.743 --> 05:07.161
All right, I'll take over.

72
05:10.540 --> 05:11.624
[grunts]

73
05:11.707 --> 05:13.000
Where should I go?

74
05:17.505 --> 05:19.924
[gentle music playing]

75
05:22.760 --> 05:24.137
[Ga-kyung moans]

76
05:29.434 --> 05:30.351
[sighs]

77
05:39.360 --> 05:40.945
Sato Hideo is in the bathroom.

78
05:42.697 --> 05:44.532
I knew she'd do something stupid.

79
05:44.615 --> 05:46.367
[moans]

80
05:48.202 --> 05:51.205
Just wait until you wake up tomorrow morning.

81
05:57.170 --> 06:00.506
She might've told Sato Hideo things she shouldn't have while drunk.

82
06:01.424 --> 06:03.259
Maybe Sato Hideo got suspicious

83
06:03.342 --> 06:05.678
and brought her all this way to keep an eye on us.

84
06:09.307 --> 06:12.643
[interesting music playing]

85
06:14.604 --> 06:16.063
[sighs deeply]

86
06:23.321 --> 06:24.697
[gunshots]

87
06:30.536 --> 06:33.581
[So-ran] Whether Sato Hideo has caught on to anything or not,

88
06:34.707 --> 06:36.042
we should find out.

89
06:45.968 --> 06:48.262
Thank you so much for today, Mr. Hideo.

90
06:48.763 --> 06:49.931
I should get going now.

91
06:50.014 --> 06:51.974
How about a drink, just the two of us?

92
06:53.184 --> 06:54.185
No, thank you.

93
06:54.268 --> 06:56.646
It's late, and I've already had a few beers.

94
06:56.729 --> 06:57.563
Well then.

95
06:57.647 --> 06:58.814
Ah…

96
07:00.274 --> 07:02.693
I didn't know you couldn't handle a few beers.

97
07:02.777 --> 07:03.611
Well, I suppose

98
07:03.694 --> 07:06.489
your arms are probably quite sore from carrying Seo-hee.

99
07:16.832 --> 07:18.501
[tense music playing]

100
07:22.630 --> 07:24.840
[in English] To our everlasting friendship.

101
07:30.513 --> 07:32.265
[in Korean] Is hard liquor not your thing?

102
07:34.725 --> 07:35.810
I'll get some beer.

103
07:35.893 --> 07:36.936
[Hideo] No, thanks.

104
07:41.816 --> 07:43.401
[laughs]

105
07:43.484 --> 07:44.777
Mr. Hideo, you know what?

106
07:47.530 --> 07:49.865
I've always wanted to get to know you better.

107
07:49.949 --> 07:51.075
Let's do that tonight.

108
08:12.597 --> 08:14.015
[Hideo sighs]

109
08:16.267 --> 08:20.104
If Seo-hee made a mistake while she was drunk earlier,

110
08:20.187 --> 08:21.731
I hope you'll forgive her.

111
08:23.024 --> 08:23.983
For my sake.

112
08:24.066 --> 08:25.234
[Philip laughs]

113
08:25.735 --> 08:27.778
She does make up some silly stories sometimes,

114
08:27.862 --> 08:29.405
but she means no harm.

115
08:30.072 --> 08:32.408
She loves to let her imagination run wild.

116
08:34.035 --> 08:35.411
[sighs] We're out of alcohol.

117
08:36.746 --> 08:38.164
Does your wife…

118
08:42.335 --> 08:43.544
know about it?

119
08:45.671 --> 08:48.132
Well, I'm sure she's caught on.

120
08:48.924 --> 08:51.719
Feelings have a way of showing, no matter how hard you hide them.

121
08:54.472 --> 08:55.473
[laughs]

122
08:56.307 --> 08:57.767
I'll go bring some beer.

123
08:59.685 --> 09:02.271
Mr. Philip, are you ever sincere about anything?

124
09:02.938 --> 09:04.607
[intriguing music playing]

125
09:07.443 --> 09:10.571
I've never been insincere for a single moment.

126
09:14.784 --> 09:15.660
It's just that…

127
09:18.329 --> 09:21.332
pretending we don't know each other's feelings

128
09:22.958 --> 09:24.585
can sometimes be for the best.

129
09:28.214 --> 09:29.090
So you mean

130
09:30.049 --> 09:31.676
your wife knows about it…

131
09:33.302 --> 09:35.429
yet you're both pretending not to know?

132
09:35.513 --> 09:36.597
[sighs in exasperation]

133
09:38.974 --> 09:40.768
I don't understand

134
09:40.851 --> 09:42.228
you people.

135
09:42.311 --> 09:44.313
Mr. Hideo, in life, there are times

136
09:45.314 --> 09:48.818
when things are too complicated to explain to others.

137
09:52.071 --> 09:55.366
After all, I've got a little more life experience than you.

138
09:59.203 --> 10:00.413
[whispers] Son of a bitch.

139
10:03.249 --> 10:04.291
Pardon me?

140
10:04.375 --> 10:06.377
[tense music playing]

141
10:22.560 --> 10:23.436
It's just that…

142
10:24.770 --> 10:27.648
pretending we don't know each other's feelings

143
10:28.649 --> 10:30.317
can sometimes be for the best.

144
10:30.943 --> 10:33.028
[metal clanging in background]

145
10:33.654 --> 10:35.531
[groaning and grunting in background]

146
10:35.614 --> 10:36.907
[glass shattering]

147
10:42.246 --> 10:43.289
[Philip] Let go!

148
10:47.376 --> 10:49.295
Do you call that complicated?

149
10:49.378 --> 10:50.796
You're just a lunatic.

150
10:51.380 --> 10:54.759
[Philip] I don't know who's the lunatic here.

151
10:58.137 --> 10:59.096
What are you doing?

152
11:00.389 --> 11:01.348
Move aside, So-ran.

153
11:01.432 --> 11:02.433
Stop it.

154
11:05.019 --> 11:06.353
[Philip grunts]

155
11:07.563 --> 11:09.940
Did I expect you to be a saint?

156
11:10.024 --> 11:10.983
Huh?

157
11:11.066 --> 11:12.443
Any decent human being would--

158
11:14.695 --> 11:16.238
Stop it already.

159
11:16.947 --> 11:18.365
Out of the way, So-ran.

160
11:18.449 --> 11:19.283
You little…

161
11:19.366 --> 11:20.242
[Philip groans]

162
11:21.744 --> 11:22.661
[scoffs]

163
11:22.745 --> 11:25.039
[both straining]

164
11:29.877 --> 11:30.836
Let this go.

165
11:31.337 --> 11:32.880
Let go.

166
11:36.800 --> 11:42.264
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

167
11:42.348 --> 11:43.182
[Ga-kyung] What?

168
11:43.265 --> 11:46.227
Wait, they got into a fistfight?

169
11:47.770 --> 11:48.896
[Ga-kyung scoffs]

170
11:48.979 --> 11:49.939
Why on earth?

171
11:50.022 --> 11:51.941
They're both grown men.

172
11:54.652 --> 11:57.488
Why did I sleep here instead of in my room?

173
11:57.571 --> 11:59.657
Sato Hideo slept in your room.

174
11:59.740 --> 12:01.408
He had too much to drink last night.

175
12:01.492 --> 12:03.494
Ah, I see.

176
12:04.620 --> 12:05.496
What?

177
12:05.579 --> 12:07.873
Sato Hideo slept in my room?

178
12:07.957 --> 12:08.999
Yes.

179
12:09.542 --> 12:10.751
[exclaims]

180
12:13.921 --> 12:14.797
Mr. Sato!

181
12:16.799 --> 12:18.634
[screams] I'm sorry!

182
12:19.301 --> 12:21.387
What do you want?

183
12:21.512 --> 12:23.722
Oh, well, it's just that…

184
12:23.806 --> 12:26.267
I came to get something.

185
12:26.850 --> 12:29.770
Can you pick that up first? Right by your feet.

186
12:31.313 --> 12:33.232
-What? -It's right by your feet.

187
12:33.315 --> 12:35.234
-On your left. -Okay.

188
12:38.153 --> 12:39.113
Here.

189
12:45.286 --> 12:46.912
Are you going to keep standing there?

190
12:46.996 --> 12:47.997
What?

191
12:48.622 --> 12:50.040
You said you came to get something.

192
12:50.958 --> 12:51.792
Oh…

193
12:51.875 --> 12:52.835
Right.

194
12:54.169 --> 12:55.045
Ah.

195
12:55.629 --> 12:56.463
Oh.

196
12:58.924 --> 13:00.301
[Ga-kyung clears throat]

197
13:01.427 --> 13:02.428
And this.

198
13:02.511 --> 13:03.345
[Ga-kyung] What?

199
13:09.393 --> 13:11.228
It looks like it got wet.

200
13:11.312 --> 13:13.897
Well, you see…

201
13:17.151 --> 13:18.402
This pawn ticket…

202
13:23.324 --> 13:24.575
belongs to my mother.

203
13:27.494 --> 13:29.121
I'm looking for my mother,

204
13:31.540 --> 13:33.459
but I don't even know her name yet.

205
13:33.542 --> 13:35.544
[mellow music playing]

206
13:35.628 --> 13:36.754
"Sin Tae-jin."

207
13:37.880 --> 13:39.340
If I find this man,

208
13:40.090 --> 13:41.842
I might be able to find my mother.

209
13:42.968 --> 13:43.802
Ah…

210
13:49.767 --> 13:50.851
[grunts]

211
13:52.394 --> 13:54.104
Keep it for the time being.

212
13:55.105 --> 13:55.939
What?

213
13:56.815 --> 13:59.485
You said you'd give it back once I found the Jongno Pawnshop.

214
14:01.487 --> 14:03.030
I will trust you.

215
14:07.493 --> 14:08.410
I'll consider you…

216
14:11.205 --> 14:12.498
my personal vault.

217
14:20.464 --> 14:23.384
Unless you're going to sew this button on, please step out.

218
14:24.593 --> 14:26.095
Sure, I'm leaving now.

219
14:26.178 --> 14:27.638
[Hideo] Aren't you going to take it?

220
14:29.515 --> 14:31.600
You said you trust me.

221
14:31.684 --> 14:32.768
[door closes]

222
14:38.983 --> 14:40.317
[groans]

223
14:41.110 --> 14:42.861
I'm completely out of it.

224
14:42.945 --> 14:44.530
[sighs deeply]

225
14:44.613 --> 14:46.991
They say when alcohol goes in, wisdom comes out.

226
14:47.866 --> 14:48.993
[sighs]

227
15:27.239 --> 15:28.824
[saucer plate clinking]

228
15:33.412 --> 15:34.371
Thank you.

229
15:36.165 --> 15:37.583
[mischievous music playing]

230
15:40.085 --> 15:42.046
[saucer plate clinking]

231
15:58.562 --> 15:59.563
[Ga-kyung clears throat]

232
16:00.230 --> 16:01.565
Excuse me, Mr. Sato.

233
16:04.568 --> 16:05.903
Interpreter Sato!

234
16:10.449 --> 16:11.533
Ah…

235
16:13.494 --> 16:18.624
Was there anything I did wrong last night?

236
16:20.334 --> 16:21.543
You see,

237
16:21.627 --> 16:25.089
I don't remember anything at all from last night.

238
16:25.881 --> 16:30.135
I hope you'll forgive me if I did anything stupid.

239
16:31.970 --> 16:33.138
[in English] Please.

240
16:35.766 --> 16:37.184
[exhales, scoffs]

241
16:37.267 --> 16:39.394
That's because you haven't sobered up yet.

242
16:39.937 --> 16:42.773
There's no way you can't remember. You're too smart to forget.

243
16:44.650 --> 16:45.859
Hmm?

244
16:45.943 --> 16:47.236
What's with the "hmm"?

245
16:48.153 --> 16:50.614
It's just a little strange.

246
16:50.697 --> 16:54.159
It's not like you to compliment me out of nowhere.

247
16:55.119 --> 16:57.996
What, have I done nothing but give you a hard time?

248
16:58.080 --> 17:00.624
You seem a little flustered right now.

249
17:03.377 --> 17:06.380
Now I'm curious, since you're acting like this all of a sudden.

250
17:06.463 --> 17:08.841
What on earth did I do last night?

251
17:08.924 --> 17:11.969
Why do you want me to remember so badly?

252
17:13.262 --> 17:15.973
Don't regret it when you remember everything.

253
17:16.557 --> 17:18.225
Then let's make a deal.

254
17:18.308 --> 17:20.853
If I remember, why don't you grant me a wish?

255
17:20.936 --> 17:22.229
Out of the blue? A wish?

256
17:22.312 --> 17:23.814
Yes, let me make a wish.

257
17:23.897 --> 17:25.566
No, I don't understand.

258
17:26.066 --> 17:28.569
You're the one who can't remember. Why do I owe you a wish?

259
17:28.652 --> 17:29.903
I need a little incentive

260
17:29.987 --> 17:31.822
to jog my memory, wouldn't you say?

261
17:32.573 --> 17:33.782
But…

262
17:33.866 --> 17:35.117
Never mind, then.

263
17:35.200 --> 17:36.994
[exclaims] Fine.

264
17:37.077 --> 17:38.745
I'll grant you one wish, then.

265
17:40.330 --> 17:42.166
But only one wish. That's it.

266
17:43.125 --> 17:45.169
It's a deal. You can't take it back.

267
17:46.170 --> 17:47.004
Geez.

268
17:50.007 --> 17:51.258
[sighs]

269
17:51.341 --> 17:53.177
What on earth did I say last night?

270
17:53.969 --> 17:56.471
I don't think I said I was a spy, so that's a relief.

271
17:57.347 --> 17:58.473
Then what was it?

272
17:59.641 --> 18:00.851
[groans]

273
18:01.393 --> 18:02.227
Ah!

274
18:02.311 --> 18:03.562
Wait for me!

275
18:03.645 --> 18:05.772
Mr. Sato, wait up!

276
18:06.815 --> 18:07.858
Let's go together.

277
18:10.861 --> 18:14.239
I think Sato Hideo has feelings for Seo-hee.

278
18:14.990 --> 18:16.325
There's no doubt about it.

279
18:17.242 --> 18:19.161
So I won't charge him for my medical bills.

280
18:20.120 --> 18:22.706
That's not a bad deal to make up for busting my lip.

281
18:23.999 --> 18:24.917
What?

282
18:25.751 --> 18:26.793
[scoffs]

283
18:31.757 --> 18:33.008
[sighs deeply]

284
18:41.475 --> 18:43.602
[telephone ringing]

285
18:47.522 --> 18:49.316
[man in Japanese] Please wait a moment.

286
18:49.399 --> 18:55.113
[speaking indistinctly]

287
18:57.282 --> 18:59.952
[in Korean] You probably don't remember anything from last night.

288
19:00.035 --> 19:02.788
You said you wanted to make lots of money to go to America.

289
19:04.623 --> 19:08.043
I sure said a lot of things.

290
19:08.126 --> 19:11.672
Practicing by yourself is nothing compared to using a language in real life.

291
19:11.755 --> 19:14.091
-Today is a fairly relaxed setting. -[pipes hissing]

292
19:14.174 --> 19:16.969
So imagine you're in America and practice.

293
19:17.052 --> 19:18.720
-Oh. -[in English] Mr. Laurent.

294
19:22.849 --> 19:25.936
This is Ms. Keum Seo-hee, who will translate for you today.

295
19:26.561 --> 19:29.439
This is Mr. Laurent, a pneumatic tube technician.

296
19:30.357 --> 19:31.817
Pleasure to meet you, Mademoiselle.

297
19:31.900 --> 19:34.278
Please lend an ear to my voice.

298
19:35.320 --> 19:36.697
I will do my best!

299
19:36.780 --> 19:38.031
[both laugh]

300
19:38.115 --> 19:42.452
The official unveiling of the pneumatic tube system is fast approaching.

301
19:42.536 --> 19:45.414
So today we'll start pre-operation inspections.

302
19:45.497 --> 19:49.209
Keijo Post Office and Government-General sections of the Joseon Line.

303
19:49.293 --> 19:50.127
Hmm?

304
19:50.961 --> 19:51.962
How's that, darling?

305
19:54.214 --> 19:55.424
Just a moment.

306
19:56.675 --> 19:59.970
What's "pneumatic tube system"?

307
20:00.053 --> 20:02.931
You can say "kisokan." The Joseon term is "gisonggwan."

308
20:05.642 --> 20:09.229
[in Japanese] The pneumatic tube system is about to be officially unveiled.

309
20:09.313 --> 20:13.317
So we're starting pre-operation inspections

310
20:13.400 --> 20:16.778
between Keijo Post Office and the Government-General.

311
20:16.862 --> 20:17.696
How's that, darling?

312
20:19.031 --> 20:20.073
Huh?

313
20:21.074 --> 20:21.908
How's that, darling?

314
20:27.122 --> 20:28.206
I see.

315
20:28.290 --> 20:29.124
Okay, then.

316
20:29.207 --> 20:30.208
Good.

317
20:30.292 --> 20:31.126
[in English] Okay.

318
20:31.209 --> 20:33.086
-[both laugh] -Oh, excuse me.

319
20:35.047 --> 20:36.214
[Hideo in Korean] Ms. Keum.

320
20:36.298 --> 20:37.132
[Ga-kyung] Yes?

321
20:39.176 --> 20:40.719
The opening ceremony next week

322
20:40.802 --> 20:43.347
is an internal event strictly for Government-General officials.

323
20:44.139 --> 20:47.351
The pneumatic tube between Keijo Post Office and the Government-General is done.

324
20:47.434 --> 20:49.936
The pneumatic tubes between here, Governor-General's residence

325
20:50.020 --> 20:51.897
on Namsan and the Inspector General's residence

326
20:51.980 --> 20:53.190
are almost finished.

327
20:53.774 --> 20:55.776
Oh, I see.

328
20:55.859 --> 21:00.364
Pneumatic tubes connect buildings over long distances with metal pipes

329
21:01.281 --> 21:04.743
and send this carrier through the pipes at high speed

330
21:05.827 --> 21:07.204
using air pressure.

331
21:07.287 --> 21:09.122
[machine whirring, pressure building]

332
21:09.206 --> 21:11.291
-[steam hissing] -[metal clangs]

333
21:11.375 --> 21:12.292
[whooshes]

334
21:12.376 --> 21:17.464
Imagine putting a dart in a hollow reed and shooting it out with a puff of air.

335
21:17.589 --> 21:19.341
Oh, I got it.

336
21:19.424 --> 21:23.178
So you can send things from place to place really fast.

337
21:23.261 --> 21:27.849
Yes. Pneumatic tubes are already common in post offices in Europe and America.

338
21:27.933 --> 21:31.103
I hear some stretch as far as the distance

339
21:31.186 --> 21:32.521
from Gyeongseong to Cheonan.

340
21:33.188 --> 21:34.147
Japan has them too.

341
21:35.273 --> 21:38.318
We're building the system around the Keijo Post Office,

342
21:38.402 --> 21:40.695
connecting the main post offices, the Government-General,

343
21:40.779 --> 21:43.156
and the Governor-General and Inspector General's residences.

344
21:43.240 --> 21:46.368
The Government-General plans to extend the network

345
21:46.868 --> 21:49.037
through Incheon, Kaesong, Pyongyang, and Sinuiju,

346
21:49.579 --> 21:52.207
and eventually all the way to Manchukuo.

347
21:52.290 --> 21:54.835
Ah, Manchukuo?

348
21:57.087 --> 21:59.047
[grunts, exhales]

349
21:59.881 --> 22:00.799
[sighs]

350
22:02.801 --> 22:04.803
I'm so glad you've come around.

351
22:05.679 --> 22:08.181
We were very worried when we got the call this morning.

352
22:08.765 --> 22:14.062
I suppose I took rather too long a stroll along the road to the afterlife.

353
22:14.146 --> 22:15.897
[Philip laughs]

354
22:17.232 --> 22:19.693
I'm glad you didn't settle down over there.

355
22:19.776 --> 22:20.777
[laughs]

356
22:22.028 --> 22:23.405
[exhales]

357
22:24.698 --> 22:25.949
[sighs]

358
22:26.032 --> 22:28.118
I heard you sent a spy into the Government-General.

359
22:29.578 --> 22:31.955
I hear it's the girl I met that day briefly.

360
22:32.038 --> 22:34.416
[panting, trembles]

361
22:34.499 --> 22:36.168
[voice shaking] It wasn't me!

362
22:37.043 --> 22:38.420
[Suzuki] Move!

363
22:38.503 --> 22:39.337
Yes.

364
22:40.338 --> 22:43.133
She doesn't know you're one of us.

365
22:44.426 --> 22:45.385
I see.

366
22:45.886 --> 22:47.012
[inhales sharply]

367
22:48.972 --> 22:52.017
I suppose the anonymous reports to the newspaper

368
22:52.100 --> 22:54.769
must've stopped while I was lying here.

369
22:54.853 --> 22:57.272
Just focus on getting better for now.

370
22:59.399 --> 23:00.942
All right.

371
23:01.026 --> 23:02.110
[inhales deeply]

372
23:02.944 --> 23:03.862
By the way…

373
23:04.404 --> 23:05.864
[sighs]

374
23:05.947 --> 23:09.159
I heard marriage talks are underway between Myung-nam

375
23:09.242 --> 23:10.785
and Sato Hideo.

376
23:12.204 --> 23:13.246
Yes.

377
23:13.330 --> 23:17.417
I'm against it. As for the reason…

378
23:20.712 --> 23:22.380
I'm sure I don't need to explain.

379
23:25.508 --> 23:26.343
Yes.

380
23:27.719 --> 23:28.845
I get it, Seog-won.

381
23:34.184 --> 23:37.479
Thank you for giving me a chance to practice interpreting.

382
23:38.230 --> 23:40.190
I know it was sudden, but you did well.

383
23:40.273 --> 23:41.149
Really?

384
23:41.691 --> 23:45.070
Ah. I'm glad you were there.

385
23:45.153 --> 23:46.238
Had I been by myself,

386
23:46.321 --> 23:48.949
I wouldn't even have known what a pneumatic tube system was.

387
23:49.532 --> 23:53.245
I actually thought the P in "pneumatic" was pronounced.

388
23:53.328 --> 23:55.455
Memorizing things really isn't enough.

389
23:55.538 --> 23:56.915
That really hit home today.

390
23:58.959 --> 23:59.793
Wait a minute.

391
24:03.004 --> 24:05.006
You just said "pneumatic" with the P, right?

392
24:09.344 --> 24:11.346
I never told you the P was silent.

393
24:14.849 --> 24:18.186
[in English] "Pneumatic

394
24:18.270 --> 24:20.397
tube system."

395
24:20.480 --> 24:22.399
[suspenseful music playing]

396
24:22.482 --> 24:25.568
[in Korean] How did you know the word "pneumatic" starts with P, not N?

397
24:30.323 --> 24:33.576
Did you go through these documents without permission?

398
24:37.872 --> 24:38.707
Yes.

399
24:40.292 --> 24:42.544
And no.

400
24:43.753 --> 24:45.797
I happened to notice them while dusting.

401
24:45.880 --> 24:48.842
There were words I didn't know, so I memorized them.

402
24:48.925 --> 24:52.095
That's how I've taught myself English all along, by picking things up.

403
24:53.138 --> 24:54.889
-And… -And?

404
24:58.351 --> 25:01.271
They seemed like words that might be related to your work,

405
25:01.354 --> 25:02.689
so I thought I'd learn them.

406
25:02.772 --> 25:05.525
In case I ever got a chance to help you,

407
25:06.151 --> 25:08.028
I wanted to do well.

408
25:10.030 --> 25:11.573
Why are you working so hard?

409
25:12.532 --> 25:13.950
He has your back anyway.

410
25:16.453 --> 25:18.121
But we might break up someday.

411
25:21.666 --> 25:24.461
Then I'll be kicked out of the Government-General.

412
25:25.295 --> 25:27.005
I'd have to find another job.

413
25:29.215 --> 25:30.091
To be honest,

414
25:32.093 --> 25:35.388
I don't want to go back to being a live-in maid.

415
25:42.395 --> 25:45.023
[police sirens wailing in background]

416
25:48.651 --> 25:49.736
[tense music playing]

417
25:49.819 --> 25:52.739
[in Japanese] Earlier today, at 11:50 a.m.,

418
25:53.406 --> 25:57.744
during the celebration of the Emperor's birthday and Japan's victory,

419
25:57.827 --> 25:59.954
held at Shanghai's Hongkou Park,

420
26:00.663 --> 26:03.291
a Josenjing named Yun Bong-gil

421
26:03.375 --> 26:04.876
threw a bomb at the gathering!

422
26:06.795 --> 26:09.714
They're still dealing with the aftermath.

423
26:10.507 --> 26:13.927
The commander of the forces stationed in Shanghai was gravely wounded.

424
26:14.010 --> 26:16.429
Many people were killed or wounded.

425
26:17.347 --> 26:19.265
The culprit was arrested at the scene,

426
26:20.141 --> 26:23.186
but it's still unclear who was behind it.

427
26:23.770 --> 26:26.689
It's likely the self-proclaimed government orchestrated it,

428
26:28.191 --> 26:30.026
but the Gugukdan might be behind it.

429
26:32.028 --> 26:33.655
The inspector general

430
26:34.864 --> 26:37.784
hasn't managed to wipe out the Gugukdan yet.

431
26:38.410 --> 26:41.454
So that's certainly possible.

432
26:48.044 --> 26:49.879
Please forgive us, Your Excellency!

433
26:50.797 --> 26:52.257
Please forgive us!

434
26:58.721 --> 26:59.681
Inspector General.

435
27:00.723 --> 27:01.558
Yes, sir.

436
27:02.350 --> 27:04.561
Postpone the pneumatic tube opening ceremony.

437
27:09.941 --> 27:10.900
[Shinichi] Sir.

438
27:11.734 --> 27:13.820
We've already invited the foreign press.

439
27:13.903 --> 27:16.072
How will you take responsibility

440
27:16.656 --> 27:20.368
if security is compromised in front of the foreign correspondents?

441
27:21.327 --> 27:22.412
[screams] You!

442
27:23.037 --> 27:26.374
We suffered terrible humiliation when the Kyeam-ri riot

443
27:26.458 --> 27:28.042
reached the foreign press in 1919.

444
27:28.126 --> 27:30.378
Have you learned nothing from that?

445
27:31.087 --> 27:31.921
Your Excellency.

446
27:32.797 --> 27:36.843
News that Yun Bong-gil, the Shanghai bomber, is Josenjing

447
27:36.926 --> 27:39.220
will make headlines around the world.

448
27:40.096 --> 27:41.306
That said,

449
27:41.389 --> 27:44.726
if word gets out that we're putting off the opening ceremony,

450
27:44.809 --> 27:46.644
it'll only make things worse for us.

451
27:51.065 --> 27:52.150
Now's the time

452
27:52.984 --> 27:54.611
to turn this situation to our advantage.

453
27:57.071 --> 27:59.949
We should hold the opening ceremony as planned on a grand scale

454
28:00.033 --> 28:02.076
to show the Government-General remains strong.

455
28:02.160 --> 28:03.703
Using event security as justification,

456
28:03.786 --> 28:05.872
we'll round up the anti-Japanese activists

457
28:06.456 --> 28:07.874
on our watch list.

458
28:13.588 --> 28:15.965
[So-ran in Korean] A celebratory banquet for the opening?

459
28:16.549 --> 28:20.261
Are they inviting foreign journalists and their spouses to the banquet?

460
28:20.345 --> 28:23.348
Yes. There'll be senior officials from the Government-General

461
28:23.431 --> 28:25.558
and pro-Japanese Joseon people, like the president

462
28:25.642 --> 28:26.976
of Joil Textile Factory that day.

463
28:31.189 --> 28:34.609
The pneumatic tube opening ceremony was supposed to be a small event,

464
28:34.692 --> 28:36.069
with only me and a few reporters.

465
28:36.152 --> 28:37.612
After today's bombing in Shanghai,

466
28:37.695 --> 28:40.573
it seems they intend to scale up the event and host a banquet

467
28:40.657 --> 28:42.784
to present Joseon to the world as a peaceful nation.

468
28:44.160 --> 28:47.121
We can't afford to miss this opportunity.

469
28:48.748 --> 28:49.582
Right.

470
28:58.174 --> 29:00.593
CHIEF OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS

471
29:02.762 --> 29:03.972
[employee in Japanese] Hello.

472
29:09.102 --> 29:10.228
[Fukuda] Oh, Sato.

473
29:11.813 --> 29:14.023
I guess the Foreign Affairs Division

474
29:14.107 --> 29:16.234
had an emergency meeting due to the Shanghai bombing.

475
29:16.818 --> 29:17.652
Yes.

476
29:17.735 --> 29:18.987
You must have your hands full.

477
29:19.070 --> 29:21.823
You should be careful, though.

478
29:21.906 --> 29:23.700
You're the inspector general's precious son,

479
29:23.783 --> 29:27.078
yet the woman you keep around is Josenjing.

480
29:27.161 --> 29:28.496
Chief of Police.

481
29:29.622 --> 29:31.124
There's no need to be upset.

482
29:31.207 --> 29:33.334
Even if she's just a worthless Josenjing woman,

483
29:33.418 --> 29:35.253
it doesn't hurt to err on the side of caution.

484
29:35.879 --> 29:39.299
All the more so in a place like the Government-General, with so many eyes

485
29:41.843 --> 29:42.677
on you.

486
29:46.723 --> 29:49.767
So you're wary of some worthless Josenjing woman.

487
29:50.977 --> 29:53.062
You're the chief of police of the Government-General.

488
29:53.146 --> 29:56.357
I guess today's news from Shanghai has made you quite nervous.

489
30:05.992 --> 30:08.369
[dramatic music playing]

490
30:08.995 --> 30:11.164
[in Korean] The opening ceremony will take place

491
30:11.247 --> 30:12.707
in the tube room on the first floor,

492
30:12.790 --> 30:15.084
and the banquet will follow here.

493
30:19.797 --> 30:21.758
-Can I try this? -Sure, go ahead.

494
30:21.841 --> 30:22.926
I'll take four.

495
30:23.009 --> 30:24.302
[merchant] All right.

496
30:24.385 --> 30:25.678
Thank you.

497
30:25.762 --> 30:28.598
Business is slow, so I really appreciate you buying four.

498
30:30.725 --> 30:32.018
-[man] Thank you. -Sure.

499
30:52.163 --> 30:53.289
KIM SEUNG-EOK, KIM MYUNG-NAM

500
30:53.373 --> 30:54.624
[Myung-nam] How do I look, Dad?

501
30:54.707 --> 30:56.626
[Seung-eok] My dear. You look absolutely lovely.

502
30:56.709 --> 30:58.878
Sweetie, you're the prettiest girl in the world.

503
30:58.962 --> 31:00.546
The prettiest of them all.

504
31:09.222 --> 31:11.182
[in Japanese] After reading the Prime Minister's

505
31:11.265 --> 31:12.934
congratulatory message and taking photos,

506
31:13.017 --> 31:15.687
the Governor-General will proceed to the second-floor banquet hall.

507
31:15.770 --> 31:18.272
Make sure he doesn't cross paths with the guests.

508
31:19.107 --> 31:22.694
The foreign press may ask technical questions about the pneumatic tubes.

509
31:23.236 --> 31:24.153
Be fully prepared.

510
31:24.904 --> 31:26.489
Yes, understood.

511
31:26.572 --> 31:28.658
[Fukuda] Comb every inch of Jongno!

512
31:29.575 --> 31:32.537
Find any trace of those bastards, by any means necessary!

513
31:33.329 --> 31:34.330
[policemen] Yes, sir!

514
31:36.124 --> 31:37.041
[people clamoring]

515
31:37.125 --> 31:38.501
[policeman 1] Get out of the way!

516
31:41.629 --> 31:42.547
[policeman 2] Get him!

517
31:44.257 --> 31:46.259
[women screaming]

518
31:47.844 --> 31:48.845
[policeman 3] Don't move!

519
31:50.388 --> 31:52.181
-[in Korean] Go inside. -[woman whimpering]

520
31:52.265 --> 31:53.099
[man] Okay.

521
32:02.900 --> 32:04.485
PNEUMATIC TUBE OPENING CEREMONY PROGRAM

522
32:09.157 --> 32:11.534
[Ga-kyung] You'll set off a bomb at the banquet?

523
32:12.493 --> 32:14.454
No. You'll never get a bomb inside.

524
32:14.537 --> 32:16.956
They'll search every single person thoroughly that day.

525
32:17.040 --> 32:21.210
Sato Hideo's fiancée will bring the bomb in before the banquet.

526
32:22.920 --> 32:24.005
His fiancée?

527
32:24.088 --> 32:25.381
Yes.

528
32:25.465 --> 32:29.552
He'll marry the daughter of the Joil Textile Factory president, Kim Seung-eok.

529
32:32.180 --> 32:34.182
[gasps] Young lady?

530
32:34.265 --> 32:36.309
She looks so gorgeous.

531
32:36.392 --> 32:39.771
Oh, do you mean the beautiful lady?

532
32:39.854 --> 32:42.648
She's Kim Myung-nam, a member of the Gugukdan.

533
32:44.817 --> 32:49.030
She recently met the inspector general at the Government-General.

534
32:49.113 --> 32:50.198
Apparently, that day,

535
32:50.281 --> 32:53.576
she was exempted from the body search and bag inspection,

536
32:54.077 --> 32:55.828
thanks to her status as Sato Hideo's fiancée

537
32:55.912 --> 32:58.164
and the inspector general's future daughter-in-law.

538
33:00.541 --> 33:02.502
A few days ahead of the banquet,

539
33:02.585 --> 33:05.588
she'll go to the Government-General with herbal medicine for her fiancé.

540
33:10.718 --> 33:14.347
There will be 30 packets of herbal medicine in her bundle.

541
33:15.264 --> 33:18.351
The ones with the shorter string are the bombs.

542
33:19.060 --> 33:21.521
Once she passes the security check,

543
33:21.604 --> 33:24.690
can you take the bundle, pull out the bomb packets,

544
33:24.774 --> 33:26.234
and hide them somewhere?

545
33:29.862 --> 33:30.738
Yes.

546
33:52.593 --> 33:53.594
[sniffles]

547
34:02.812 --> 34:03.688
[in Japanese] Hanako.

548
34:04.605 --> 34:05.565
The other day--

549
34:05.648 --> 34:07.859
Are you sorry for yelling at me?

550
34:10.486 --> 34:11.320
Yeah.

551
34:13.030 --> 34:14.282
I'm sorry, Hanako.

552
34:15.449 --> 34:16.826
That's okay.

553
34:20.663 --> 34:22.081
[knocking on door]

554
34:26.669 --> 34:29.046
[in Korean] Hideo, I'll leave your change of clothes here.

555
34:39.724 --> 34:43.895
Your father told me to make preparations for your wedding in the summer.

556
34:50.443 --> 34:51.694
[Hideo] From now on…

557
34:54.197 --> 34:55.406
you don't have to bring those.

558
34:56.199 --> 34:57.033
Why?

559
34:58.701 --> 34:59.911
I don't want to wear them.

560
35:21.557 --> 35:25.102
[ominous music playing]

561
35:28.898 --> 35:31.025
[telephone ringing]

562
35:33.486 --> 35:35.821
[in Japanese] Hello? This is Interpreter Sato's office.

563
35:42.286 --> 35:43.621
[gasps] Over here.

564
35:43.704 --> 35:44.580
Yes.

565
35:48.709 --> 35:49.794
Hello, Ms. Kim.

566
35:50.962 --> 35:53.839
This is the daughter of Joil Textile Factory's president

567
35:53.923 --> 35:56.300
and Interpreter Sato's fiancée.

568
35:57.468 --> 36:00.471
He recognized me from when I came here last time

569
36:00.554 --> 36:03.683
to visit the inspector general with my father.

570
36:04.267 --> 36:06.602
Oh… Oh my, really?

571
36:07.812 --> 36:08.646
Well…

572
36:09.522 --> 36:10.356
Yes.

573
36:10.439 --> 36:11.774
[chuckles]

574
36:12.525 --> 36:15.069
What brings you here today?

575
36:15.653 --> 36:16.487
Ah.

576
36:16.570 --> 36:19.949
Hideo has been so busy lately,

577
36:20.032 --> 36:22.285
so I brought him some herbal medicine.

578
36:22.868 --> 36:25.830
[laughs] He's going to be thrilled.

579
36:27.123 --> 36:31.335
Uh, I'm afraid he's at a meeting at the moment.

580
36:31.419 --> 36:33.546
Would you like to wait in his office?

581
36:33.629 --> 36:34.547
Sure.

582
36:35.131 --> 36:36.882
All right. Let me…

583
36:37.675 --> 36:38.551
Right this way.

584
36:39.260 --> 36:40.428
[policeman 1] Just a moment.

585
36:40.511 --> 36:42.680
We need to search you and check your bundle.

586
36:43.264 --> 36:44.181
Huh?

587
36:46.017 --> 36:48.060
Then I'll do it.

588
36:48.144 --> 36:52.481
We'd have to wait for Ms. Abe, who searches female staff

589
36:52.565 --> 36:54.317
in the mornings.

590
36:54.400 --> 36:56.485
-[giggles] -[policeman 1] I'll take the bundle.

591
36:58.487 --> 37:00.114
[tense music playing]

592
37:04.452 --> 37:05.453
[mumbles]

593
37:06.662 --> 37:08.122
[policeman 2] Let her through.

594
37:15.379 --> 37:16.213
Here you go.

595
37:16.297 --> 37:17.340
Sorry for the trouble.

596
37:20.092 --> 37:21.385
Thank you.

597
37:21.469 --> 37:24.347
I'll be sure to let the inspector general know

598
37:24.430 --> 37:27.141
how accommodating you've been today.

599
37:27.224 --> 37:28.934
[laughs]

600
37:29.018 --> 37:30.019
I'd appreciate that.

601
37:30.102 --> 37:31.562
Thanks for your help.

602
37:32.188 --> 37:33.022
Thank you.

603
37:36.901 --> 37:38.361
-[Myung-nam gaps] -Oh!

604
37:38.444 --> 37:39.945
-[snarls] -[trembles]

605
37:40.029 --> 37:41.739
Kotaro, not that!

606
37:41.822 --> 37:43.949
-[in Korean] Why's there a dog? -I don't know.

607
37:44.033 --> 37:45.409
I've never seen it before either.

608
37:45.493 --> 37:46.869
[Ga-kyung laughs awkwardly]

609
37:46.952 --> 37:48.996
[in Japanese] Was there a sniffer dog here?

610
37:49.080 --> 37:50.623
We brought it in a little while ago.

611
37:50.706 --> 37:54.377
We're taking extra security precautions after the Shanghai bombing.

612
37:54.460 --> 37:57.880
I wonder why the sniffer dog is acting up…

613
37:57.963 --> 38:00.549
It's the smell of the herbal medicine that's bothering it.

614
38:00.633 --> 38:02.468
That makes sense.

615
38:02.551 --> 38:05.346
[in Korean] Let's go already. I'm terrified of dogs.

616
38:06.180 --> 38:07.181
Let's go.

617
38:10.726 --> 38:12.812
[both screams]

618
38:13.771 --> 38:15.189
[in Japanese] Get the dog!

619
38:15.272 --> 38:16.774
No, Kotaro! Stop!

620
38:17.817 --> 38:18.818
Come on!

621
38:20.111 --> 38:22.488
-[both straining] -[dog grunting]

622
38:22.571 --> 38:23.781
[both screaming]

623
38:27.701 --> 38:30.079
-[dog barking] -[Ga-kyung panting]

624
38:30.162 --> 38:31.872
[policeman 3] Kotaro, settle down!

625
38:32.456 --> 38:33.707
So sorry.

626
38:33.791 --> 38:35.084
[pants]

627
38:35.167 --> 38:37.837
-[dog barking continues] -[Ga-kyung panting]

628
38:50.182 --> 38:51.559
[Ga-kyung in Korean] Huh?

629
38:51.642 --> 38:52.768
That's

630
38:52.852 --> 38:54.562
a bomb detonator.

631
38:54.645 --> 38:56.397
[suspenseful music playing]

632
38:56.480 --> 38:58.357
[in Japanese] Why's it barking so much?

633
39:02.528 --> 39:03.696
Uh…

634
39:10.327 --> 39:11.245
Oh my goodness!

635
39:11.328 --> 39:13.456
Miss, your ring fell.

636
39:13.539 --> 39:15.166
Oh no, my ring!

637
39:37.980 --> 39:41.358
[dog barking]

638
39:47.573 --> 39:48.491
What's going on?

639
39:49.950 --> 39:51.035
Mr. Sato!

640
39:52.953 --> 39:56.665
Your fiancée brought you some herbal medicine.

641
40:03.005 --> 40:04.131
Ah…

642
40:04.215 --> 40:07.426
He picked up the ring for you.

643
40:10.054 --> 40:12.306
[in Korean] Don't show up out of the blue again.

644
40:13.265 --> 40:14.475
Thanks, but I can't take that.

645
40:15.643 --> 40:17.353
No, I insist. Please, take it--

646
40:17.436 --> 40:19.230
No, please take it back.

647
40:22.107 --> 40:24.193
[in English] There are too many eyes on us now.

648
40:24.276 --> 40:26.403
It would be insulting to the lady.

649
40:26.487 --> 40:28.405
[dog barking continues]

650
40:32.117 --> 40:34.078
[in Korean] Then, I'll take just this.

651
40:40.834 --> 40:42.962
[policeman 2 in Japanese] Something is off.

652
40:45.756 --> 40:48.259
The dog is barking at you, Ms. Keum.

653
40:50.553 --> 40:51.762
Ah…

654
40:52.596 --> 40:56.058
Maybe I put on too much ointment.

655
40:56.141 --> 40:58.310
I've got quite a few bruises.

656
41:05.776 --> 41:07.611
We'll have to search you.

657
41:08.195 --> 41:09.738
Call Ms. Abe.

658
41:10.614 --> 41:12.074
-This way. -Huh?

659
41:15.953 --> 41:16.870
Uh…

660
41:17.371 --> 41:19.999
[in Korean] I put on the ointment you bought me, though.

661
41:34.054 --> 41:35.222
[in Japanese] And now

662
41:35.973 --> 41:37.224
it's barking at me.

663
41:40.644 --> 41:42.396
The dog must be a little agitated.

664
41:43.147 --> 41:44.440
[policeman 2 laughs]

665
41:44.523 --> 41:46.317
It seems so.

666
41:47.318 --> 41:48.193
Yamamoto.

667
41:48.277 --> 41:49.486
Get the dog out of here!

668
41:49.570 --> 41:50.404
Yes, sir!

669
41:54.158 --> 41:55.159
Thanks, carry on.

670
41:58.037 --> 41:58.871
Take care, then.

671
42:11.425 --> 42:12.426
[sighs]

672
42:22.227 --> 42:23.395
[in Korean] Uh…

673
42:23.479 --> 42:26.148
I see your meeting finished early.

674
42:28.859 --> 42:30.194
Are you badly bruised?

675
42:31.153 --> 42:33.238
-Sorry? -You said you used the ointment.

676
42:33.947 --> 42:36.408
Ah. Yes, but I'm fine.

677
42:36.492 --> 42:38.410
I've put on the ointment you got me.

678
42:38.494 --> 42:39.536
I'll be better soon.

679
42:39.620 --> 42:41.121
Where is it this time?

680
42:42.581 --> 42:43.540
Let me have a look.

681
42:44.917 --> 42:48.170
If you keep getting hurt, you might end up with scars.

682
42:48.671 --> 42:49.505
Where is it?

683
42:49.588 --> 42:50.923
Is it your wrist again?

684
42:51.006 --> 42:51.840
No.

685
42:52.675 --> 42:53.509
It's…

686
42:56.428 --> 42:59.264
somewhere I can't show you.

687
43:00.140 --> 43:02.101
[soft music playing]

688
43:07.231 --> 43:08.774
I'd forgotten for a moment.

689
43:46.937 --> 43:48.897
How could you be with him?

690
44:02.494 --> 44:03.412
What are you doing

691
44:04.455 --> 44:05.289
so close to me?

692
44:06.665 --> 44:07.583
It's just

693
44:08.292 --> 44:10.043
you had some dust on you.

694
44:16.592 --> 44:18.761
Why on earth do you keep seeing a man like that?

695
44:19.386 --> 44:20.220
What?

696
44:20.304 --> 44:22.890
He's abusive toward you, isn't he?

697
44:24.767 --> 44:28.187
He's supposed to treat you like a princess.

698
44:29.563 --> 44:32.065
Treat me like a princess?

699
44:33.567 --> 44:35.027
Where did that come from? [chuckles]

700
44:38.489 --> 44:40.282
[Hideo] I'm sorry. I'm so sorry.

701
44:40.365 --> 44:42.117
[Ga-kyung sighs loudly] That's weird.

702
44:42.785 --> 44:44.620
Why's the ground moving?

703
44:46.038 --> 44:47.206
Stay still!

704
44:47.289 --> 44:48.499
Ms. Keum.

705
44:49.458 --> 44:51.126
Ms. Keum, try to sober up.

706
44:51.210 --> 44:53.170
-Keum Seo-hee-- -Keum Seo-hee?

707
44:54.379 --> 44:55.672
[laughs]

708
44:56.840 --> 44:58.592
But I'm not Keum Seo-hee.

709
44:58.675 --> 45:00.594
[giggles]

710
45:01.720 --> 45:02.763
No, I'm not.

711
45:02.846 --> 45:04.723
Yeah, of course.

712
45:04.806 --> 45:06.350
Ms. Lee Gae-ttong.

713
45:06.934 --> 45:08.435
We're almost there.

714
45:09.978 --> 45:10.812
Right.

715
45:12.397 --> 45:13.774
-[Hideo grunts] -[Ga-kyung groans]

716
45:14.358 --> 45:15.484
Ah!

717
45:15.567 --> 45:16.485
It's that way.

718
45:16.568 --> 45:18.570
No, not that way. This way.

719
45:19.112 --> 45:21.156
Now, here.

720
45:22.449 --> 45:23.283
Get on my back.

721
45:23.951 --> 45:24.952
What?

722
45:25.035 --> 45:26.245
I said get on my back.

723
45:28.872 --> 45:29.957
I don't want to.

724
45:30.040 --> 45:30.874
What?

725
45:32.209 --> 45:33.335
[exhales]

726
45:34.836 --> 45:36.505
Hold me in your arms.

727
45:37.798 --> 45:38.632
What?

728
45:39.383 --> 45:40.509
I mean,

729
45:40.592 --> 45:44.888
I want you to hold me like this too.

730
45:44.972 --> 45:48.016
Like you did on the train.

731
45:51.853 --> 45:52.688
Why?

732
45:53.397 --> 45:54.356
You don't want to?

733
45:56.441 --> 45:57.568
Ah…

734
45:58.610 --> 46:03.407
I'm having an affair with a married man.

735
46:03.490 --> 46:07.077
So I guess I don't deserve to be treated like a princess.

736
46:08.287 --> 46:09.288
[sighs deeply]

737
46:09.871 --> 46:11.331
[cries]

738
46:11.415 --> 46:13.208
That's not it…

739
46:13.292 --> 46:14.126
Are you crying?

740
46:16.336 --> 46:18.297
Just this once.

741
46:19.089 --> 46:19.965
Please?

742
46:21.300 --> 46:23.594
Come on, hold me.

743
46:25.512 --> 46:27.306
Like a princess.

744
46:29.099 --> 46:30.100
[sighs]

745
46:30.976 --> 46:31.977
[Ga-kyung] Prince.

746
46:34.021 --> 46:35.188
[gasps softly]

747
46:39.109 --> 46:41.903
I'm afraid I don't know what you mean by princess.

748
46:42.613 --> 46:43.947
Now

749
46:44.906 --> 46:45.741
you remember.

750
46:45.824 --> 46:46.700
No.

751
46:47.200 --> 46:48.535
I don't remember anything.

752
46:48.619 --> 46:52.247
Mmm. Yes, you do. You look like you remember perfectly.

753
46:52.331 --> 46:55.208
No, I don't remember a thing, I swear.

754
46:55.292 --> 46:58.712
You promised to grant me a wish if I remembered.

755
46:58.795 --> 47:00.464
Why would I pass up that chance?

756
47:01.048 --> 47:02.174
I mean it.

757
47:07.638 --> 47:08.472
Fine.

758
47:09.431 --> 47:11.558
I hope you never remember.

759
47:12.726 --> 47:15.896
I'll definitely remember, so you'd better keep your promise,

760
47:16.480 --> 47:17.898
Interpreter Sato Hideo.

761
47:22.319 --> 47:23.737
Very well.

762
47:25.322 --> 47:26.156
I'll be waiting.

763
47:27.824 --> 47:28.825
Hmm.

764
47:39.127 --> 47:42.130
You can't leave that American, even though he mistreats you.

765
47:45.384 --> 47:47.636
Is it really to keep this job?

766
47:53.350 --> 47:58.146
My personal life is none of your business.

767
48:02.901 --> 48:06.655
By the way, I heard you're getting married to that lady from earlier.

768
48:09.658 --> 48:11.076
You two make a great couple.

769
48:11.159 --> 48:12.035
Congratulations.

770
48:12.119 --> 48:13.161
My marriage…

771
48:15.914 --> 48:17.958
is none of your business, Ms. Keum.

772
48:26.717 --> 48:28.510
Go home if you're done for the day.

773
48:31.722 --> 48:32.723
[door opens]

774
48:34.850 --> 48:36.059
[door closes]

775
48:40.313 --> 48:41.314
[sighs]

776
49:02.419 --> 49:06.047
LADIES' ROOM

777
49:20.187 --> 49:22.314
[mischievous music playing]

778
49:36.578 --> 49:38.663
[sighs] That'll do.

779
49:39.581 --> 49:42.042
[groans, exhales]

780
49:42.125 --> 49:45.128
There's no better place to hide a bomb than the ladies' room,

781
49:45.212 --> 49:47.631
because hardly anyone comes in here.

782
49:53.428 --> 49:55.430
[sighing]

783
49:55.514 --> 49:56.681
Finally.

784
49:57.349 --> 49:58.725
It all worked out.

785
50:06.233 --> 50:07.609
[Hideo] My marriage

786
50:07.692 --> 50:09.653
is none of your business, Ms. Keum.

787
50:12.531 --> 50:13.406
[clears throat]

788
50:14.616 --> 50:16.201
It all turned out as it should.

789
50:17.911 --> 50:19.287
[sighs]

790
50:19.371 --> 50:21.289
It's got nothing to do with me.

791
50:31.758 --> 50:32.926
[squeaking]

792
50:37.514 --> 50:38.515
[sighs]

793
50:52.529 --> 50:54.406
Oh, my teacher is here.

794
50:54.489 --> 50:55.782
Hi, Kkot-nim.

795
50:56.366 --> 50:57.325
Have you been well?

796
50:57.409 --> 51:00.203
Yes, but we don't have a lesson today.

797
51:00.871 --> 51:01.746
You're right.

798
51:03.498 --> 51:07.627
I just wanted to study with you, so I thought I'd stop by.

799
51:08.336 --> 51:09.921
Kkot-nim, who's that?

800
51:10.881 --> 51:13.008
He teaches me how to read hangul.

801
51:13.508 --> 51:14.467
Teacher.

802
51:14.551 --> 51:16.469
This is Geum-ok.

803
51:16.553 --> 51:17.679
Oh…

804
51:18.513 --> 51:19.890
-Hello. -Hello.

805
51:22.809 --> 51:23.852
You teach her how to read?

806
51:23.935 --> 51:25.145
You scared me.

807
51:30.191 --> 51:32.110
Could you read this letter for me?

808
51:32.903 --> 51:35.614
My little brother went to Manchuria to find work.

809
51:35.697 --> 51:37.699
The one who used to read for me is no longer here.

810
51:38.742 --> 51:40.493
Could you read this for me, please?

811
51:40.577 --> 51:41.745
Sure, I will.

812
51:49.878 --> 51:51.171
I did open it.

813
51:51.254 --> 51:54.257
I've tried to make sense of it, but I can't read.

814
51:55.008 --> 51:56.468
What does it say?

815
51:57.218 --> 51:59.095
Does it say he's coming back?

816
51:59.179 --> 52:01.014
It's already been three years since he left.

817
52:01.097 --> 52:02.307
Uh…

818
52:02.390 --> 52:04.559
It's about money…

819
52:05.602 --> 52:06.603
Will he send me money?

820
52:07.812 --> 52:09.064
He wants you to send him money.

821
52:09.898 --> 52:10.732
Pardon?

822
52:13.276 --> 52:14.736
[groans]

823
52:15.946 --> 52:17.447
I'm going to wring his neck.

824
52:18.239 --> 52:19.574
-[whips] -[No-ma groaning]

825
52:19.658 --> 52:21.493
Sorry, please forgive me.

826
52:23.662 --> 52:24.496
[Mun-mak] Hey!

827
52:25.205 --> 52:27.082
You got fired from your delivery job.

828
52:27.624 --> 52:30.877
Then pull a rickshaw or something.

829
52:30.961 --> 52:34.881
Or pick the pockets of those rich men in suits!

830
52:35.674 --> 52:37.384
Forgive me, I'll do better from tomorrow--

831
52:37.467 --> 52:38.301
Shut up!

832
52:39.344 --> 52:40.929
-Shut your mouth! -[grunting]

833
52:41.012 --> 52:42.597
[Hideo] What do you think you're doing?

834
52:48.395 --> 52:49.646
-Are you okay? -[panting]

835
52:51.523 --> 52:53.274
Why would you beat him?

836
52:54.109 --> 52:54.985
He's--

837
52:55.068 --> 52:58.113
Hey, shut up and get lost.

838
53:04.452 --> 53:05.912
Well, well, this young master

839
53:05.996 --> 53:07.539
[breathes heavily]

840
53:07.622 --> 53:10.125
is sticking his nose into other people's business.

841
53:11.501 --> 53:15.296
Or should I call you the son of the inspector general?

842
53:16.798 --> 53:18.299
Don't be so surprised.

843
53:18.925 --> 53:21.720
You're quite well-known around Gyeongseong.

844
53:22.887 --> 53:24.139
Yet that idiot Lee No-ma…

845
53:25.140 --> 53:27.017
[exhales] doesn't even know who you are.

846
53:28.184 --> 53:30.186
Lee No-ma would lose his damn mind

847
53:30.270 --> 53:32.647
whenever the Japanese are involved.

848
53:33.440 --> 53:36.651
Funny how he even stands guard while you hand out your charity.

849
53:36.735 --> 53:38.903
[laughs]

850
53:41.698 --> 53:43.616
Watch yourself, young master.

851
53:43.700 --> 53:47.871
Plenty of idiots around here got crippled fighting for independence.

852
53:49.164 --> 53:52.667
You don't want to draw their attention by poking around here.

853
53:53.585 --> 53:54.419
What?

854
53:55.628 --> 53:57.005
Do you think this is bullshit?

855
53:59.257 --> 54:01.551
Some people around here are from Kyeam-ri.

856
54:02.594 --> 54:04.387
What would happen if they saw you?

857
54:04.929 --> 54:08.099
Wouldn't they want to burn you alive just the same?

858
54:09.100 --> 54:10.894
[ominous music playing]

859
54:10.977 --> 54:12.854
What do you mean?

860
54:14.814 --> 54:16.399
"Burn you alive just the same"?

861
54:16.483 --> 54:18.276
Kyeam-ri, you know.

862
54:20.779 --> 54:22.072
Don't you know Kyeam-ri?

863
54:26.701 --> 54:28.119
[Yamada in Japanese] Ms. Keum.

864
54:28.203 --> 54:30.205
-Are you going home? -Well, yes.

865
54:30.288 --> 54:33.666
Come with me. There's an important update regarding the banquet.

866
54:33.750 --> 54:37.962
CAMELLIA CLUB

867
54:38.046 --> 54:40.632
[ambient music playing]

868
54:44.260 --> 54:45.386
[Hideo] I like you.

869
54:48.932 --> 54:49.891
I like you,

870
54:50.892 --> 54:52.060
Ms. Keum.

871
54:54.354 --> 54:55.522
[thuds]

872
54:55.605 --> 54:57.357
[breathes heavily]

873
54:59.192 --> 55:01.444
What was the important update?

874
55:05.824 --> 55:08.952
Is the rumor true that Interpreter Sato is getting married?

875
55:09.536 --> 55:11.204
-What? -Word's gotten around.

876
55:11.287 --> 55:13.373
I heard his fiancée came to visit this afternoon.

877
55:13.998 --> 55:15.875
Is he really getting married?

878
55:16.459 --> 55:17.627
Ah…

879
55:17.710 --> 55:19.170
Uh…

880
55:19.254 --> 55:20.088
Yes.

881
55:22.590 --> 55:25.051
A man like him deserves

882
55:25.135 --> 55:27.262
so much better than a Joseon woman!

883
55:29.556 --> 55:32.642
If that's all, I'll get going…

884
55:32.725 --> 55:33.560
[glass thuds]

885
55:34.394 --> 55:35.395
Today has been

886
55:36.437 --> 55:38.731
absolutely the worst day ever.

887
55:39.983 --> 55:41.067
Earlier today,

888
55:41.151 --> 55:45.363
a mouse fell out of the ladies' room's ceiling vent

889
55:45.446 --> 55:48.575
and landed right on my shoulder.

890
55:48.658 --> 55:50.910
I screamed and nearly jumped out of my skin.

891
55:51.411 --> 55:53.454
I don't want to talk about it anymore.

892
55:53.538 --> 55:55.582
Once they set a trap tomorrow,

893
55:55.665 --> 55:57.250
they'll catch the mouse eventually.

894
55:57.333 --> 56:00.670
But Mr. Sato's marriage…

895
56:00.753 --> 56:03.548
[cries]

896
56:03.631 --> 56:06.009
Today's been so awful for me, right?

897
56:06.885 --> 56:07.719
Don't you think?

898
56:08.469 --> 56:09.596
[Ga-kyung laughs]

899
56:09.679 --> 56:10.513
Yes.

900
56:11.598 --> 56:13.099
I'd say so.

901
56:13.183 --> 56:15.226
[cries]

902
56:19.647 --> 56:20.815
Hold on.

903
56:21.774 --> 56:23.484
A mouse trap?

904
56:23.568 --> 56:24.652
Yeah.

905
56:24.736 --> 56:28.615
They'll have it set up first thing in the morning

906
56:28.698 --> 56:30.742
inside the ladies' room vent.

907
56:30.825 --> 56:32.118
[exclaims]

908
56:32.202 --> 56:34.954
In the vent tomorrow morning?

909
56:35.038 --> 56:37.373
-Yeah, early, before office hours-- -[screams]

910
56:37.457 --> 56:39.292
I have an emergency at home!

911
56:39.375 --> 56:40.501
[Yamada] Wait.

912
56:40.585 --> 56:41.836
[Ga-kyung] I'm sorry!

913
56:41.920 --> 56:43.463
[Yamada] Ms. Keum!

914
56:43.546 --> 56:45.506
[whimpers]

915
56:45.590 --> 56:47.133
What's gotten into her?

916
56:48.176 --> 56:51.346
Oh no, Hideo!

917
56:51.429 --> 56:55.225
RECORDS ROOM

918
56:57.602 --> 56:58.561
[in Korean] Kyeam-ri.

919
57:00.063 --> 57:01.731
GYEONGGI POLICE DEPARTMENT REPORT FILE

920
57:01.814 --> 57:02.857
Kyeam-ri.

921
57:04.275 --> 57:05.109
[sighs]

922
57:11.658 --> 57:12.575
1919

923
57:18.373 --> 57:19.374
Kyeam-ri.

924
57:20.041 --> 57:21.376
[Ga-kyung in Japanese] Please.

925
57:21.459 --> 57:23.586
I left something very important inside.

926
57:23.670 --> 57:24.587
We can't allow it.

927
57:24.671 --> 57:27.632
You need a permit to reenter the Government-General

928
57:27.715 --> 57:28.675
after office hours.

929
57:30.134 --> 57:32.637
But Ms. Yamada was allowed in without a permit.

930
57:32.720 --> 57:34.430
I saw her when I was leaving work.

931
57:34.514 --> 57:35.598
Ms. Yamada?

932
57:36.724 --> 57:37.600
Ah…

933
57:38.643 --> 57:39.477
That's right.

934
57:40.061 --> 57:41.062
So?

935
57:41.145 --> 57:42.564
But you are…

936
57:44.440 --> 57:46.109
Josenjing.

937
57:47.944 --> 57:50.488
Come back during office hours tomorrow.

938
57:51.489 --> 57:53.157
[stammers] But…

939
57:53.241 --> 57:54.659
[Fukuda] What's going on?

940
57:54.742 --> 57:57.954
Ms. Keum from the Foreign Affairs Division is requesting reentry after hours.

941
58:00.331 --> 58:02.000
Reentry, you say?

942
58:03.293 --> 58:04.627
What did you leave behind?

943
58:04.711 --> 58:07.964
The foreign press translation about the pneumatic tube opening ceremony.

944
58:08.590 --> 58:12.010
Interpreter Sato said he'd go over it first thing tomorrow morning.

945
58:12.093 --> 58:13.720
I meant to finish it at home.

946
58:16.347 --> 58:18.975
Please. Allow me to go back in.

947
58:22.604 --> 58:23.688
Fine.

948
58:24.689 --> 58:25.523
You may come in.

949
58:26.566 --> 58:27.900
Thank you.

950
58:27.984 --> 58:28.943
On one condition.

951
58:32.739 --> 58:33.990
Go through here.

952
58:39.370 --> 58:40.330
Why?

953
58:41.372 --> 58:42.206
You can't do it?

954
58:44.459 --> 58:48.588
You want a favor, so show some respect as a Josenjing.

955
58:49.672 --> 58:51.758
[sinister music playing]

956
59:01.017 --> 59:01.851
Come on.

957
59:18.951 --> 59:19.952
[in French] Mademoiselle?

958
59:25.541 --> 59:28.211
[in English] I was just on my way to find you.

959
59:30.046 --> 59:32.757
Do you mind if I borrow the lady for a moment?

960
59:40.598 --> 59:41.432
[clears throat]

961
59:41.516 --> 59:43.893
You said you wish to speak with Mitarai-san?

962
59:43.976 --> 59:45.603
I'll just stop at the restroom--

963
59:45.687 --> 59:47.271
That was just an excuse.

964
59:49.023 --> 59:52.110
Look. Whatever you're up to, I won't ask.

965
59:52.860 --> 59:54.070
But in return, please,

966
59:54.696 --> 59:56.030
have a date with me sometime.

967
59:56.114 --> 59:58.449
-Hmm? -Uh…

968
01:00:00.034 --> 01:00:01.035
[in French] Bonne soirée.

969
01:00:03.162 --> 01:00:04.539
[stammers in English] Thank you!

970
01:00:06.624 --> 01:00:07.875
[gasps softly]

971
01:00:11.671 --> 01:00:12.964
[pants]

972
01:00:17.093 --> 01:00:19.846
MAY 4, 1919, CHIEF OF SUWON POLICE STATION THE KYEAM-RI RIOT

973
01:00:26.769 --> 01:00:28.104
KEUM SEOK-JUNG

974
01:00:34.193 --> 01:00:35.987
[Seok-jung in Japanese] You broke her leg?

975
01:00:36.571 --> 01:00:38.990
[screams] You damn monsters!

976
01:00:39.073 --> 01:00:40.575
[gunshot echoes]

977
01:00:44.579 --> 01:00:46.330
[gloomy music playing]

978
01:00:57.967 --> 01:01:01.637
KILLING OF 23 JOSEON PEOPLE AT KYEAM AND VILLAGE DESTRUCTION BY JAPANESE SOLDIERS

979
01:01:06.934 --> 01:01:08.811
JOSEON PEOPLE'S 'NON-VIOLENT REVOLUTION' REPORT

980
01:01:41.886 --> 01:01:45.097
STATEMENT OF LIEUTENANT ISHIKAWA

981
01:01:52.104 --> 01:01:53.815
[Shinichi] What do you think you're doing?

982
01:01:54.941 --> 01:01:56.901
ISHIKAWA NOBUYUKI

983
01:02:13.292 --> 01:02:15.044
[music fades]

984
01:02:29.851 --> 01:02:32.228
[in Korean] So in the end, you chose to do something stupid.

985
01:02:44.824 --> 01:02:45.825
[screams] Father!

986
01:02:49.662 --> 01:02:52.748
Hold on, where should I hide this?

987
01:02:56.627 --> 01:02:57.795
[breathes heavily]

988
01:02:57.879 --> 01:02:58.880
Not here.

989
01:03:00.673 --> 01:03:01.507
Maybe here?

990
01:03:01.591 --> 01:03:02.967
That's even riskier.

991
01:03:04.594 --> 01:03:06.012
[sighs in frustration]

992
01:03:08.806 --> 01:03:10.224
INTERPRETER SUZUKI HIROYUKI

993
01:03:14.395 --> 01:03:15.229
[Hideo] Father.

994
01:03:16.272 --> 01:03:18.065
[footsteps approaching]

995
01:03:18.149 --> 01:03:19.108
Oh!

996
01:03:20.568 --> 01:03:23.738
[exclaims]

997
01:03:34.916 --> 01:03:36.000
Father.

998
01:03:39.962 --> 01:03:42.298
Is this man, Ishikawa, really you?

999
01:03:42.381 --> 01:03:44.342
Did you really…

1000
01:03:45.968 --> 01:03:47.720
kill 23 Joseon people in Kyeam-ri?

1001
01:03:53.059 --> 01:03:54.060
Did you shoot them,

1002
01:03:54.977 --> 01:03:56.145
set them on fire,

1003
01:03:57.730 --> 01:03:58.898
and behead them?

1004
01:04:01.108 --> 01:04:01.943
Really?

1005
01:04:03.653 --> 01:04:05.237
What is it you want to tell me?

1006
01:04:06.447 --> 01:04:09.367
Are you here to condemn me for killing Joseon people?

1007
01:04:10.117 --> 01:04:11.077
Father.

1008
01:04:14.455 --> 01:04:16.123
Do you remember

1009
01:04:16.832 --> 01:04:20.252
when I shot a Joseon boy in front of you, saying he was a pickpocket?

1010
01:04:22.171 --> 01:04:24.423
But you were the real pickpocket.

1011
01:04:25.758 --> 01:04:27.009
Did you ever challenge me

1012
01:04:27.551 --> 01:04:30.137
for killing an innocent boy back then?

1013
01:04:32.473 --> 01:04:33.557
No, you never did.

1014
01:04:34.642 --> 01:04:35.726
Instead, you…

1015
01:04:38.938 --> 01:04:40.272
[policeman in Japanese] Go away.

1016
01:04:40.356 --> 01:04:41.190
Get out of here!

1017
01:04:44.443 --> 01:04:48.114
[breathing shakily]

1018
01:04:49.907 --> 01:04:51.575
[in Korean] Will you come with me?

1019
01:04:54.745 --> 01:04:57.164
I'm asking if you'll become my son.

1020
01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:02.586
[dramatic music playing]

1021
01:05:21.439 --> 01:05:23.482
[Shinichi] You took the hand I reached out to you.

1022
01:05:24.316 --> 01:05:26.986
You chose to become Japanese. No one forced you.

1023
01:05:27.486 --> 01:05:29.572
You changed your name to save yourself

1024
01:05:30.239 --> 01:05:33.117
and survived because an innocent child died in your place.

1025
01:05:35.286 --> 01:05:37.121
So why are you acting like a fool?

1026
01:05:38.289 --> 01:05:41.709
Is that Josenjing blood still running through your veins, Sato Hideo?

1027
01:05:50.342 --> 01:05:52.428
If you can't forget where you came from,

1028
01:05:53.512 --> 01:05:56.515
remember who gave you this life of luxury all this time.

1029
01:05:58.434 --> 01:05:59.810
However foolish you may be,

1030
01:06:00.853 --> 01:06:02.772
you should know you owe me some gratitude.

1031
01:06:10.696 --> 01:06:14.450
[door opens, slams shut]

1032
01:06:29.131 --> 01:06:30.424
[gasps]

1033
01:06:32.676 --> 01:06:34.220
Uh…

1034
01:06:34.303 --> 01:06:35.429
Mr. Sato.

1035
01:06:37.515 --> 01:06:38.933
How long have you been here?

1036
01:06:40.017 --> 01:06:40.851
That's…

1037
01:06:41.560 --> 01:06:42.394
Get out.

1038
01:06:47.441 --> 01:06:49.235
[screams] I said, get out of here, now!

1039
01:06:54.156 --> 01:06:55.366
[sighs]

1040
01:06:57.451 --> 01:06:58.869
Did you find that amusing?

1041
01:07:01.038 --> 01:07:03.207
I said I didn't know

1042
01:07:04.375 --> 01:07:06.377
whether to side with Joseon or Japan.

1043
01:07:07.837 --> 01:07:09.255
Do you think I deserve this?

1044
01:07:14.093 --> 01:07:14.927
Get out.

1045
01:07:16.929 --> 01:07:19.014
[screams] Get out of my sight right now!

1046
01:07:28.607 --> 01:07:30.734
Mr. Sato, listen.

1047
01:07:33.863 --> 01:07:34.989
[Ga-kyung] At that moment,

1048
01:07:36.574 --> 01:07:38.367
I remembered everything.

1049
01:07:39.118 --> 01:07:41.996
I remember now.

1050
01:07:47.001 --> 01:07:48.711
I remember

1051
01:07:50.504 --> 01:07:51.922
what I said to you.

1052
01:07:54.383 --> 01:07:55.759
You know what?

1053
01:07:56.343 --> 01:07:57.511
[Hideo] Yes?

1054
01:07:58.137 --> 01:08:00.097
Would you let me call you

1055
01:08:00.181 --> 01:08:03.225
by your Joseon name?

1056
01:08:06.187 --> 01:08:07.521
[sighs]

1057
01:08:07.605 --> 01:08:10.608
Kim Tae-ung suits you

1058
01:08:10.691 --> 01:08:14.445
so much better than Sato Hideo.

1059
01:08:15.821 --> 01:08:17.489
May I call you that?

1060
01:08:20.159 --> 01:08:21.160
[low groan]

1061
01:08:22.036 --> 01:08:24.038
I'm asking you.

1062
01:08:25.998 --> 01:08:27.249
[in English] Please.

1063
01:08:29.627 --> 01:08:32.087
[emotional music playing]

1064
01:08:32.171 --> 01:08:33.130
[in Korean] So…

1065
01:08:35.299 --> 01:08:36.342
please don't cry.

1066
01:08:46.810 --> 01:08:48.979
I'm asking you to grant my wish.

1067
01:08:53.692 --> 01:08:54.735
Don't cry,

1068
01:08:56.612 --> 01:08:58.113
Interpreter Kim Tae-ung.

1069
01:09:47.162 --> 01:09:51.083
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

1070
01:09:51.166 --> 01:09:53.252
[So-ran] The day of the opening ceremony.

1071
01:09:53.335 --> 01:09:55.254
-We'll retrieve the three bombs -[woman screams]

1072
01:09:55.337 --> 01:09:57.464
we've smuggled in and make time bombs.

1073
01:09:57.548 --> 01:10:00.634
His name was Keum Seok-jung, wasn't it?

1074
01:10:00.718 --> 01:10:03.095
[Philip] This one's a time bomb. You can make it out alive.

1075
01:10:03.804 --> 01:10:05.723
[So-ran] You'll be leaving tomorrow night.

1076
01:10:06.974 --> 01:10:08.475
[Hideo] Say that again.

1077
01:10:08.559 --> 01:10:10.436
-What did you say? -[gun clicks]

1078
01:10:10.519 --> 01:10:13.063
[Ga-kyung] I think it's time I gave this back to you.

1079
01:10:13.564 --> 01:10:15.524
[Hideo] I know my own name perfectly well.

1080
01:10:15.607 --> 01:10:17.609
[Ga-kyung] You have your own path,

1081
01:10:17.693 --> 01:10:19.862
and I have mine. We each have our own path.

1082
01:10:19.945 --> 01:10:21.322
[Hideo] Hold on.

1083
01:10:21.905 --> 01:10:23.115
What's this here?

1084
01:10:29.371 --> 01:10:31.373
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
